<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> clean the base, drip and iron on a wax matched to the snow temperature, let it cool fully, then scrape it thin with a plastic scraper and brush the base along its length. Plan on waxing every 4 to 6 ski days, or whenever the base looks dry and white.</p></div>

<h2>Why waxing matters</h2>
<p>Wax helps your skis glide, protects the base from drying out, and makes turning feel smoother and more predictable. A dry base feels slow and sticky, especially in spring snow.</p>

<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wax:</strong> a universal or temperature-specific <%= gear_link "ski wax", "ski wax" %>. Universal wax works across a wide range of conditions; temperature-specific wax is better when you know the forecast.</li>
  <li><strong>A waxing iron:</strong> a dedicated <%= gear_link "ski waxing iron", "ski wax iron" %> holds a steady temperature. A household iron can scorch the base and shouldn't be used for food or clothes afterward.</li>
  <li><strong>A plastic scraper</strong> and a <strong>brush</strong> (nylon or horsehair for finishing). Many come together in a <%= gear_link "ski tuning kit", "ski tuning kit" %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Ski vises or a stable surface,</strong> and something to hold the brakes up, such as a thick rubber band.</li>
  <li><strong>Base cleaner</strong> or a clean rag, if the base is dirty.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Choosing a wax</h2>
<p>Wax is labeled by temperature range. Match it to the snow temperature you'll ski in. Colder snow is more abrasive and needs harder wax; warm, wet snow needs softer wax. Check the forecast on the <%= link_to "resort pages", resorts_path %> before you wax for a trip. If you only buy one, buy universal.</p>

<h2>Step-by-step: hot waxing</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Set up.</strong> Secure the ski base-up in vises or on a stable bench, and hold the brakes up out of the way.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean the base.</strong> Wipe off dirt. For a very dirty base, use base cleaner and let it dry fully.</li>
  <li><strong>Heat the iron.</strong> Set it to the temperature on the wax packaging. The wax should melt smoothly without smoking. If it smokes, the iron is too hot.</li>
  <li><strong>Drip the wax.</strong> Hold the wax against the iron and drip a line down each side of the base, tip to tail.</li>
  <li><strong>Iron it in.</strong> Move the iron steadily from tip to tail in long, overlapping passes. Keep it moving; never let it sit in one spot. A thin, even layer of melted wax should trail behind the iron.</li>
  <li><strong>Let it cool.</strong> Leave the skis at room temperature until the wax is fully hardened, usually at least 30 minutes. Longer is better.</li>
  <li><strong>Scrape.</strong> With a sharp plastic scraper held at an angle, scrape tip to tail until only a thin, almost invisible layer remains. Clear wax off the edges and sidewalls too.</li>
  <li><strong>Brush.</strong> Brush tip to tail to open the base structure and polish. The base should look smooth and slightly glossy.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Quick alternatives</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rub-on or paste wax:</strong> fast and portable for a mid-trip boost, but it wears off quickly.</li>
  <li><strong>Shop wax:</strong> most ski shops offer a hot wax or full tune. A full tune also sharpens edges and repairs the base, which is worth doing once or twice a season.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How often to wax</h2>
<p>A common rule is every 4 to 6 days on snow. Wax sooner if the base looks dry or white along the edges, if the skis feel slow, or after skiing abrasive man-made or very cold snow. If you rent, the shop handles it; see <%= link_to "rent or buy ski gear", guide_path("rent-or-buy-ski-gear") %>.</p>
<p>Snowboarder? See <%= link_to "how to wax a snowboard", guide_path("how-to-wax-a-snowboard") %>.</p>
